================================================================================
AROL AGENTIC TELEMETRY ANALYSIS
System and Device Programming - Project Q3
Multi-Agent System (MAS) for Industrial IoT Data Refinement and Analytics
================================================================================

WHAT THIS IS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A chatbot that analyses capping-machine telemetry, built so that the language
model never touches the arithmetic.

A user asks a question in plain language ("what is the success rate per head?",
"is anything wrong with head 26?"). An orchestrator agent decides which
analysis tools to call and in what order; deterministic Python functions do
every calculation; a report assembler writes the answer around the numbers.

The model chooses tools. It never sees a raw telemetry row and never computes
a value. That is what makes the output repeatable: the same question returns
the same numbers every time, and a test asserts it.


REQUIREMENTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Python 3.11 or newer, plus:

    pandas, numpy, scipy, pyyaml, pyarrow, matplotlib, pytest

Install with:

    pip install -r requirements.txt

No API key and no network access are needed for anything described below: the
default planner is rule-based. The LLM planner is optional (see STEP 4).


HOW TO RUN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
All commands are run from the repository root.

  List the analysis tools the agent can call:
      python -m src.interface.cli tools

  List the available data pools:
      python -m src.interface.cli pools

  Generate a canned report (kpi | anomalies | idle | throughput):
      python -m src.interface.cli report kpi
      python -m src.interface.cli report anomalies

  Ask a question in plain language:
      python -m src.interface.cli ask "is anything wrong with the machine?"
      python -m src.interface.cli ask "idle periods on head 5"
      python -m src.interface.cli ask "throughput by hour"

  Interactive session:
      python -m src.interface.cli chat

  Global options (these go BEFORE the subcommand):
      --config PATH   use a different config file
      --pool NAME     analyse a specific pool
      --no-save       print only; do not write report and trace files

Reports are written to reports/ and the matching tool-call trace to logs/.


RUNNING THE TESTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      python -m pytest tests -q

The suite covers the integration contract (schema, status decoding, envelope,
registry), the KPI tools, the agent loop including its failure paths, and a
ground-truth evaluation that measures anomaly detection against faults
deliberately injected into the synthetic data.


DATA
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Two sources, selected by one key in config.yaml:

    data:
      source: synthetic     # or: real

  synthetic  Generated events with faults injected at known locations. Used
             for development and as the ground truth for evaluation. Needs no
             telemetry files. This is the default.

  real       Person A's WP1 loader over the AROL CSV files. Point
             data.pools at the day-files first.

Everything else is identical between the two: both produce the same event
table, and the same tests run against either.

INPUT FORMAT (real source)
  One CSV per machine per day, ~57 MB, 86,400 rows (one sample per second).
  Columns: a single "timestamp", then three per head - "H## Count",
  "H## AppTorque", "H## Status" - for 36 heads (109 columns in total).
  Head count is auto-detected, not hard-coded.

  A closure is a +1 increment of a head's counter. Its torque and status sit
  on the same row as the increment. Files in a pool are read in timestamp
  order and treated as ONE continuous counter stream, so a closure that
  straddles midnight is still detected.

EVENT TABLE (what every analysis tool consumes)
  One row per closure event:

    ts             plant-local naive timestamp of the increment
    pool_id        which pool the event came from
    machine_id     parsed from the filename
    head_id        H01 .. H36
    head_index     1 .. 36, for numeric sort order
    torque         AppTorque on the increment row, Nm
    status         raw status code
    error_class    decoded name (Closure OK / No Load / Bad Closure / Unknown)
    reject_signal  status bit 0
    cap_present    a cap was actually applied
    count_delta    counter increment that produced this event
    inferred       True when count_delta > 1 (a dropped poll)

STATUS CODES
  Observed in the real data: 0, 2 and 65 only.

    0    Closure OK    a cap was applied successfully, torque about 2.0 Nm
    2    No Load       the head cycled with no cap present, torque about 0
    65   Bad Closure   a reject; rare (single digits per file)

  Decoded as a bitfield rather than an enum: bit 0 is the reject flag and the
  high bits are the error category, so 65 == 64 | 1. Unseen codes decode to
  "Unknown (<code>)" and are marked unconfirmed instead of crashing.

  IMPORTANT: the success rate uses cap-present closures as its denominator.
  A No Load cycle is not a failed closure - there was no cap to close - so
  counting it as a failure understates performance badly. Reports always
  state which denominator was used and give both numbers.


CONFIGURATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Everything lives in config.yaml; nothing is hard-coded. Copy it to
config.local.yaml to override values locally without touching the shared file
(config.local.yaml is gitignored).

    data.source        synthetic | real
    data.pools         ordered day-files per pool
    data.timezone      plant-local timezone the timestamps are recorded in
    analytics.*        thresholds (sigma, idle window, min_n, torque range)
    agent.planner      rules | llm
    agent.model        model id used by the LLM planner


REPOSITORY LAYOUT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    config.yaml            all paths, thresholds and parameters
    src/common/            schema, envelope, registry, config, time helpers
    src/ingestion/         Person A: loader, validation, closure detection
    src/analytics/         analysis tools (Person B: KPI half)
    src/agent/             orchestrator, planner, report assembler, trace log
    src/interface/         CLI
    src/testing/           synthetic generator with injected faults
    tests/                 test suite
    docs/                  contract, audit, architecture notes
    reports/               generated reports    (gitignored)
    logs/                  generated traces     (gitignored)


DIVISION OF WORK
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Person A   ingestion (WP1), statistical tools: torque distributions,
               drift detection, correlation, torque-based anomalies
    Person B   KPI tools, orchestrator, planner, report assembler, trace log,
               CLI and chat, synthetic generator, integration tests
    Both       the integration contract in docs/contract.md, tool schemas,
               agent prompts, the demo and the presentation

The two halves meet at the event table and the tool registry, both defined in
docs/contract.md. Neither side may change that shape alone.


STATUS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Working now:
    event-table schema and validation, status decoding, tool registry with
    generated JSON schemas, five KPI tools, the rule-based planner, the
    orchestrator loop with retry and graceful degradation, the report
    assembler, the trace log, the CLI, the synthetic generator with ground
    truth, and the test suite.

Not yet built:
    Person A's real loader (src/ingestion/api.py is a stub), his statistical
    tools, the LLM planner, plots, HTML and PDF export, and the scaling
    benchmark.
